Set that action's value to `"none"` under the `keybinds` object in your `100xprompt.json`. This unbinds it entirely, so no key triggers it:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://100xprompt.com/config.json",
  "keybinds": {
    "session_compact": "none"
  }
}
```

Include only the actions you want to change. Any action you leave out keeps its default binding.

If you change your mind later, assign the action a key again and it works immediately. Several actions already ship unbound (`none`) by default and work the same way.

To have the change apply in every project, put it in your **global** `100xprompt.json` rather than a project-level config.

Set the action to `"none"` in the `keybinds` object in `100xprompt.json` to unbind it entirely:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://100xprompt.com/config.json",
  "keybinds": {
    "session_new": "none"
  }
}
```

Hi,

The docs’ “everything you leave out keeps its default” rule applies to actions you omit from `keybinds`. Since you added `app_exit`, you overrode that action’s binding, replacing its default `ctrl+c,ctrl+d,<leader>q` with only `ctrl+w`.

To keep the defaults and add your new shortcut, use a comma-separated list for `app_exit`:

```json
{
  "keybinds": {
    "app_exit": "ctrl+c,ctrl+d,<leader>q,ctrl+w"
  }
}
```

Separating alternatives with commas binds several shortcuts to the same action, so this keeps the default exit bindings and adds `ctrl+w`.

Best,
100xprompt Support
